Title: Make the R23 acceptance test use authentic cross-version indexes and prove continuity
Created: 2026-09-04
Author: codex
Blocked-by: 0623

--- log ---
2026-09-04T10:05Z codex created

--- body ---

## Context

Child of goal-2 tracker `0614`. R23 requires an index actually written under a
different schema version to end up serving in both directions, without manual
file deletion. The current target-neutral check edits only
`meta.schemaVersion` on a current-format database, reopens it with the same
binary, and calls any non-empty hit list success. That is neither an authentic
upgrade nor an authentic rollback, and it can pass after partial loss, fallback
service, or a silent same-path rebuild — the last of which R23 explicitly calls
a failure.

Ticket `0623` owns three prerequisite defects already measured at the reviewed
baseline: restoring a main SQLite file while stale WAL/SHM companions survive,
using schema 0 rather than the reachable predecessor rung, and dropping partial
arm evidence from `not-run`. This ticket does not duplicate them. It starts from
their correction and owns whether the resulting experiment actually measures
R23.

## Required experiment

Use authentic, immutable cross-version fixtures. The upgrade fixture is
produced by the immediately preceding supported writer and consumed by the
current binary. The downgrade fixture is produced by the current writer and
consumed by the predecessor binary that represents a real rollback. Record the
producer and consumer revisions, schema stamps, fixture hashes, and complete
SQLite snapshot membership. A label edited onto a current schema is a negative
control, not an acceptance fixture.

Seed discriminating content whose stable item identities, passage identities,
text hashes, vector presence and library partition can be compared before and
after. The post-transition query must return the seeded identities through the
index under test and identify the backend/generation that answered. A count or
an arbitrary non-empty result is insufficient.

Prove continuity rather than availability: preserve the fixture's required
rows and payloads, show no fresh crawl/rebuild supplied the answer, and detect
truncation, overwrite or atomic replacement under the same filename. Legitimate
target-managed SQLite transitions — checkpointing WAL, removing SHM, renaming a
migration temporary file — are not "manual deletion"; operator/harness actions
must be recorded directly instead of inferred from a disappearing path.

If migration is asynchronous, wait on a target-owned terminal observable under
a bound owned by `SPEC.md`; do not grade one immediate query as the eventual
state. Bind the damage-prevention and serving halves in the same artifact by
fixture hash, arm, producer, consumer and target revision.

## Red-first controls

- a current-layout database carrying only a forged foreign stamp is refused as
  an authentic fixture
- an in-place truncate-and-rebuild under the same filename fails
- a fresh-index or live-Zotero fallback returning non-empty hits fails
- a migration returning one seeded hit while losing other seeded rows fails
- an answer from the wrong library partition fails
- a target-managed WAL checkpoint or temporary-file cleanup does not fail as
  manual deletion
- an authentic predecessor migration that preserves and serves the seed passes
- an authentic rollback path passes only when it preserves service; a typed
  incompatibility response is useful diagnostic evidence but is a non-pass for
  R23 unless a ruling recorded in `DECISIONS.md` first changes that reading; the
  arm is never simulated by the current binary meeting stamp 9999

## Exit criteria

- [ ] 0623's coherent reset, reachable predecessor and `not-run` evidence fixes
      are present
- [ ] both arms use immutable fixtures written by the stated producer binaries
- [ ] the downgrade arm executes the stated older consumer binary
- [ ] fixture hashes and complete SQLite snapshot membership reach the artifact
- [ ] stable seeded identities and required payload preservation are compared,
      not only hit counts
- [ ] the artifact proves the answer came from the transitioned index and not a
      fallback or fresh rebuild
- [ ] same-path overwrite, truncation and silent rebuild are detected
- [ ] manual harness intervention is recorded directly; legitimate target file
      transitions do not manufacture a red
- [ ] asynchronous completion is observed through a target-owned terminal state
- [ ] damage prevention and serving continuity are bound to the same arms and
      revisions
- [ ] every red-first control above is exercised by the normal gate or by a
      named integration gate that cannot silently disappear
- [ ] a real zoteus run produces an honest verdict for both directions
- [ ] tracker 0614 and README record only the resulting measured standing
- [ ] `make check` passes
